ST-T1024 MCU Getting Started Guide
Build Your First Deterministic AI Prototype


Objective
This guide shows how to move from the ST-T1024 specification to a first working MCU prototype.


The goal:
Run → Understand → Implement → Measure


1. Choose Your Platform
Supported paths:
ARM Cortex-M
RISC-V MCU
FPGA prototype
Minimum:
32-bit processor
Embedded memory
Debug interface


2. Prepare the Environment
Install:
Compiler toolchain
Debug tools
Python emulator environment
Git


3. Clone the Repository
git clone https://github.com/actuing/ST-T1024-STANDARD-FOR-EMBEDDED-LLM.git


4. Run the Reference Emulator
Before hardware:
python3 improved_st_t1024_v2.1.py
Verify:
deterministic execution
FSM sequence
tile routing
output consistency


5. Build the Minimal MCU Version
Start with:
FSM scheduler
One domain tile
Ternary operations
Input/output interface
Do not begin with the complete 1024-tile system.


6. First Hardware Test
Measure:
Flash usage
SRAM usage
Execution cycles
Power consumption
Record:
MCU model
Clock frequency
Compiler version
Results


7. Move to the Full Implementation Guide
After the first prototype:
→ ARM optimization
→ RISC-V optimization
→ Custom instructions
→ Power gating
→ Certification analysis



First Mission
One MCU. One engineer. One working prototype.

The first implementation creates the foundation for everything that follows.

This document would act as the "front door" specifically for embedded engineers, while the larger MCU Implementation Guide remains the technical reference manual.



